If you want to link your C/C++ code against libgretl you need:

1. unpack mscompile.zip
2. install Microsoft Visual Studio (2022 used) or MS SDK
2. install native MS Windows Clang/LLVM compiler

**Note:** Currently, MSVC's `cl.exe` cannot be used due to differences in how MSVC defines
complex types. We are actively working on resolving this issue.

Simple examples you'll find in c/c++ directories. Note, that in order to execute produced
binaries you must set some environmental variables (see setenv.bat or setenv.ps1 for details).

Using Different C Runtimes Simultaneously,
If you are mixing code linked against MSVCRT and UCRT, ensure that memory allocation and deallocation occur within the same C runtime. Specifically, memory allocated using malloc() in MSVCRT must not be freed using free() from UCRT, and vice versa. Failing to do so will result in a segmentation fault (SIGSEGV), which cannot be mitigated.
To address this issue, we provide special functions in libgretl, such as gretl_list_free(), to handle memory management safely.

